Common-pool starvation

  // BAD: parallel stream that does blocking IO
  List<User> fetched = ids.parallelStream()
                          .map(id -> httpClient.get(id))  // blocks common-pool thread
                          .toList();
  // 8-core machine -> 7 common-pool threads
  // 7 concurrent HTTP calls -> the rest of the JVM cannot use commonPool()

  // GOOD: dedicated ForkJoinPool for the blocking work
  ForkJoinPool ioPool = new ForkJoinPool(32);
  List<User> fetched = ioPool.submit(() ->
      ids.parallelStream().map(id -> httpClient.get(id)).toList()
  ).join();

  // Or better: virtual threads
  try (var ex = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
      List<Future<User>> futures = ids.stream()
          .map(id -> ex.submit(() -> httpClient.get(id)))
          .toList();
      // collect results
  }

Splittability

  FAST split: ArrayList, arrays, IntStream.range, ConcurrentSkipListMap
  SLOW split: LinkedList, Stream.iterate, BufferedReader.lines, HashSet

When to use

  CPU-bound + large + splittable + no IO + no locks + order-independent
    -> parallel stream (one line)

  IO-bound OR small OR order-dependent OR lock-heavy
    -> keep sequential, or use async / virtual threads

Deadlock

  T1: lock A, wait B    T2: lock B, wait A
  -> cycle -> jstack shows 'Found one Java-level deadlock'
  -> BLOCKED threads, low CPU

  Fix 1: lock ordering
    if (from.id < to.id) { lock(from); lock(to); }
    else                 { lock(to); lock(from); }

  Fix 2: tryLock with timeout
    if (lockA.tryLock(100, MS)) {
        try {
            if (lockB.tryLock(100, MS)) {
                try { /* work */ } finally { lockB.unlock(); }
                return;
            }
        } finally { lockA.unlock(); }
        // timeout -> back off and retry
    }

Livelock

  T1 holds A, tries B, fails, releases A, retries
  T2 holds B, tries A, fails, releases B, retries
  -> both retry in lockstep forever (RUNNABLE, high CPU, no progress)

  Fix: randomized backoff
    Thread.sleep(ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(10, 50));
    // or lock ordering, which removes the cycle entirely

Starvation

  ReentrantLock unfair = new ReentrantLock(false);  // default -- can starve
  ReentrantLock fair   = new ReentrantLock(true);   // FIFO, no starvation, slower

  Thread pool with priority queue and a constant stream of high-priority tasks
    -> low-priority tasks never run

  Fix: fair locks where fairness matters; bounded queues for backpressure;
       do not rely on thread priorities (most JVMs ignore them)

wait/notify -- three bugs

  BUG 1: waiting with if
    synchronized (lock) {
        if (!ready) lock.wait();  // WRONG -- spurious wakeup returns from wait
        useReadyValue();
    }

  FIX 1: wait in a while loop
    synchronized (lock) {
        while (!ready) lock.wait();
        useReadyValue();
    }

  BUG 2: lost wakeup
    // notifier runs notify() before waiter calls wait()
    // waiter misses the notification and blocks forever

  FIX 2: hold the lock while changing the condition AND notifying
    synchronized (lock) { ready = true; lock.notifyAll(); }

  BUG 3: notify() wakes the wrong waiter
    // one reader is woken when a writer is needed -> lost wakeup

  FIX 3: notifyAll() (safe default); notify() only if you can prove correctness

Modern alternative

  BlockingQueue<Task> q = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(1000);
  q.put(task);       // no wait/notify code
  Task t = q.take();

  CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(3);
  Condition notFull = lock.newCondition();
  // all handle spurious wakeups, lost wakeups, and fairness internally

Virtual thread basics

  // create and start
  Thread vt = Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -> {
      Thread.sleep(1000);            // parks the virtual thread, not the carrier
      System.out.println("done");
  });

  // executor (the recommended pattern)
  try (var ex = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
      List<Future<Result>> futures = IntStream.range(0, 10_000)
          .mapToObj(i -> ex.submit(() -> callRemote(i)))
          .toList();
      for (Future<Result> f : futures) f.get();
  }

Pinning (Java 21 and earlier)

  // BLOCKS THE CARRIER -> no benefit from virtual threads
  synchronized (lock) {
      Thread.sleep(1000);   // virtual thread pinned to the carrier
  }

  // OK: ReentrantLock does not pin
  lock.lock();
  try {
      Thread.sleep(1000);   // carrier is released, another VT runs
  } finally {
      lock.unlock();
  }

  // Detect pinning
  //   -Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full
  //   JFR event: jdk.VirtualThreadPinned

  // Java 24 (JEP 491): synchronized no longer pins

Carrier pool

  Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors() -> carriers
  -- parallelism is bounded by carriers
  -- thousands of virtual threads, dozens of carriers

Do not pool

  // BAD: pooling virtual threads defeats the purpose
  ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(100);  // platform

  // GOOD: new virtual thread per task
  ExecutorService vt = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor();

ThreadLocal interaction

  private static final ThreadLocal<Request> CTX = new ThreadLocal<>();

  try (var ex = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
      ex.submit(() -> {
          CTX.set(request);        // scoped to THIS virtual thread
          try { handle(); }
          finally { CTX.remove(); } // good practice -- not strictly required
      });
  }
  // next task -> new virtual thread -> CTX is fresh (no leak, no reuse)

