CountDownLatch (wait for N services)

  CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(3);
  executor.submit(() -> { try { svcA(); } finally { latch.countDown(); } });
  executor.submit(() -> { try { svcB(); } finally { latch.countDown(); } });
  executor.submit(() -> { try { svcC(); } finally { latch.countDown(); } });

  latch.await(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS);   // blocks until all 3 done (or timeout)
  // aggregate results

CyclicBarrier (iterative phases)

  CyclicBarrier barrier = new CyclicBarrier(N, () -> mergeResults());
  for (int t = 0; t < N; t++) {
      final int id = t;
      executor.submit(() -> {
          for (int phase = 0; phase < P; phase++) {
              computePhase(id, phase);
              barrier.await();      // sync with other threads each phase
          }
      });
  }

Semaphore (limit concurrent calls to an external API)

  Semaphore limit = new Semaphore(10, true);  // 10 concurrent, fair

  Result callExternal(Request r) throws InterruptedException {
      limit.acquire();
      try {
          return externalClient.call(r);
      } finally {
          limit.release();   // MUST release, or permits leak to zero
      }
  }

  // Rate limiting pattern: N permits, 1 permit per call
  // Resource pool:   N permits for N connections

Backpressure (three layers)

  // 1. bounded queue + blocking put (backpressure)
  BlockingQueue<Task> q = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(1000);
  q.put(task);   // blocks when full -- producer slows down

  // 2. fast rejection when full (shed load)
  if (!q.offer(task)) {
      return Response.status(429).header("Retry-After", "1").build();
  }

  // 3. circuit breaker on the downstream
  @CircuitBreaker(name = "external")
  Result call() { return externalClient.call(); }

  // All three together: bounded queue, fast 429, circuit breaker

Structured concurrency (Java 21 preview)

  // ShutdownOnFailure: fan-out all-of, cancel siblings on first failure
  Response handleOrder(Order order) throws Exception {
      try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope.ShutdownOnFailure()) {
          Subtask<User>       user  = scope.fork(() -> userSvc.get(order.userId()));
          Subtask<List<Item>> items = scope.fork(() -> itemSvc.byOrder(order.id()));
          Subtask<Price>      price = scope.fork(() -> priceSvc.compute(order));

          scope.join();           // wait for all (or first failure)
          scope.throwIfFailed();  // throws if any subtask failed

          return new Response(user.get(), items.get(), price.get());
      }
      // on scope close, any still-running subtasks are cancelled
  }

  // ShutdownOnSuccess: any-of, cancel siblings on first success
  try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope.ShutdownOnSuccess<String>()) {
      scope.fork(() -> cache.get(key));
      scope.fork(() -> db.get(key));
      scope.join();
      return scope.result();
  }

  // Structured concurrency guarantees:
  //   - no orphaned child threads after the scope exits
  //   - cancellation propagates to children
  //   - exceptions from children are surfaced to the parent
  //   - ScopedValue context is inherited by children

Decision guide

  wait for N tasks to complete              -> CountDownLatch
  iterate with N threads per phase          -> CyclicBarrier
  limit concurrent access to a resource     -> Semaphore
  producer-consumer with pressure           -> bounded BlockingQueue + 429/503
  fan-out/fan-in with cancellation          -> StructuredTaskScope

Broken without happens-before

  // Thread A
  x = 1;
  flag = true;   // non-volatile

  // Thread B
  if (flag) {
      int y = x;  // may see y = 0 (write not visible)
  }

Fixed with volatile

  volatile boolean flag;
  int x;

  // Thread A
  x = 1;         // write before volatile write
  flag = true;   // volatile write -- publishes x

  // Thread B
  if (flag) {    // volatile read
      int y = x; // GUARANTEED to see x = 1
  }

  Why: volatile write happens-before volatile read, and
       program order gives x = 1 --> flag = true.
       By transitivity: x = 1 happens-before y = x.

Fixed with synchronized

  // Thread A
  synchronized (lock) {
      x = 1;
      flag = true;
  }

  // Thread B
  synchronized (lock) {
      if (flag) { int y = x; }   // sees x = 1
  }

  Why: monitor release happens-before monitor acquire
       on the same lock.

count++ is NOT atomic

  volatile int count;
  // three steps: read count, add 1, write count
  // two threads can both read 0, both write 1 -- lost increment

  AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger();
  count.incrementAndGet();   // atomic CAS

  // or
  synchronized (this) { count++; }

The eight happens-before rules

  1. Program order      within a thread, actions are ordered
  2. Monitor lock       release of monitor hb acquire of same monitor
  3. Volatile           write to volatile hb subsequent read
  4. Thread start       Thread.start() hb every action in started thread
  5. Thread join        every action in thread hb join() return
  6. Transitivity       A hb B, B hb C  =>  A hb C
  7. Interruption       Thread.interrupt() hb interrupted-thread sees it
  8. Final fields       correct construction publishes finals safely
                       (no reference to this escaping during construction)

ThreadLocal leak (recap)

  private static final ThreadLocal<Ctx> CTX = new ThreadLocal<>();

  // BAD: pooled thread keeps the value after the task
  void handle() { CTX.set(load()); /* no remove */ }

  // GOOD
  try { CTX.set(load()); /* work */ }
  finally { CTX.remove(); }

MDC across threads (the failure)

  // request thread
  MDC.put("traceId", "abc123");
  executor.submit(() -> {
      log.info("handling");   // logs WITHOUT traceId -- worker thread has empty MDC
  });

  // fix 1: capture and set MDC in the worker
  Map<String, String> ctx = MDC.getCopyOfContextMap();
  executor.submit(() -> {
      Map<String, String> prev = MDC.getCopyOfContextMap();
      if (ctx != null) MDC.setContextMap(ctx);
      try { log.info("handling"); }
      finally {
          if (prev != null) MDC.setContextMap(prev);
          else MDC.clear();
      }
  });

  // fix 2: Spring TaskDecorator (auto-propagates MDC)
  class MdcTaskDecorator implements TaskDecorator {
      public Runnable decorate(Runnable r) {
          Map<String, String> ctx = MDC.getCopyOfContextMap();
          return () -> {
              try {
                  if (ctx != null) MDC.setContextMap(ctx);
                  r.run();
              } finally { MDC.clear(); }
          };
      }
  }

  @Bean ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor() {
      ThreadPoolTaskExecutor ex = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
      ex.setTaskDecorator(new MdcTaskDecorator());
      return ex;
  }

  // fix 3: tracing framework (recommended)
  // spring-boot-starter-actuator + micrometer-tracing-bridge-otel
  // + opentelemetry-exporter-otlp -- trace context propagates via
  //   traceparent headers, MDC is populated automatically from the
  //   current span (spring.micrometer.tracing.* flags)

ScopedValue (Java 21 preview)

  static final ScopedValue<RequestContext> REQ = ScopedValue.newInstance();

  void handleRequest(HttpRequest req) {
      RequestContext ctx = buildContext(req);
      ScopedValue.where(REQ, ctx).run(() -> {
          doWork();                          // REQ.get() is available here
          // child structured tasks also see REQ (if structured concurrency)
      });
      // outside the block, REQ is not set -- no leak
  }

  // immutable: no set after bind
  // bounded: cleared on scope exit
  // inherited: structured child tasks can read the parent's ScopedValue

Propagation decision guide

  same thread, short scope              -> RequestContextHolder / ThreadLocal
  same thread, async boundaries         -> TaskDecorator / explicit context
  distributed trace context             -> Micrometer Tracing / OpenTelemetry
  new code, ScopedValue available       -> ScopedValue
  legacy code, no async                  -> ThreadLocal with remove-in-finally

