JNI (traditional)

  // Java side
  public class NativeCompressor {
      static { System.loadLibrary("compress"); }
      public native byte[] compress(byte[] input);
  }

  // C side (compress.c)
  JNIEXPORT jbyteArray JNICALL Java_NativeCompressor_compress
      (JNIEnv* env, jobject self, jbyteArray input) {
      jsize len = (*env)->GetArrayLength(env, input);
      jbyte* buf = (*env)->GetByteArrayElements(env, input, NULL);
      // ... compress ...
      jbyteArray out = (*env)->NewByteArray(env, outLen);
      (*env)->SetByteArrayRegion(env, out, 0, outLen, outBuf);
      (*env)->ReleaseByteArrayElements(env, input, buf, JNI_ABORT);
      return out;
  }

  // Pitfalls: memory safety, GlobalRef, thread attach, ExceptionCheck,
  // boundary overhead per call.

FFM (Java 22+, Project Panama)

  import java.lang.foreign.*;
  import java.lang.invoke.*;

  // look up the native function
  Linker linker = Linker.nativeLinker();
  SymbolLookup compress = SymbolLookup.libraryLookup("libcompress.so", Arena.global());
  MethodHandle compressFn = linker.downcallHandle(
      compress.find("compress").orElseThrow(),
      FunctionDescriptor.of(ValueLayout.ADDRESS, ValueLayout.ADDRESS, ValueLayout.JAVA_LONG));

  // use an Arena for deterministic lifetime
  try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined()) {
      MemorySegment inputSeg = arena.allocate(input.length);
      inputSeg.copyFrom(MemorySegment.ofArray(input));

      MemorySegment outSeg = (MemorySegment) compressFn.invoke(inputSeg, input.length);
      byte[] result = new byte[(int) outSeg.byteSize()];
      MemorySegment.copy(outSeg, 0, result, 0, result.length);
      return result;
  }
  // arena.close() frees the native memory deterministically

  // bounds-checked: out-of-bounds access throws IndexOutOfBoundsException
  // lifetime-checked: using a segment after its arena is closed throws
  // no native glue, no JNI headers, pure Java

Virtual thread pinning during JNI

  // Java 21 and earlier: pinned during native calls and synchronized
  // Java 24 (JEP 491): no pinning inside synchronized
  // native calls still pin -- prefer non-blocking native libraries
  // or migrate to FFM when available

Practical rules

  - coarse-grained calls (compress 1 MB, hash large array, query DB)
  - one JNI/FFM call per operation, not per element
  - prefer FFM on Java 22+ when the library supports it
  - JNI still needed for older JDKs, unmigrated libraries, native
    libraries that expose complex Java-facing behavior
  - remember: a native segfault kills the whole JVM

