ServiceTalk mental model

  Application code
     |
     v
  ServiceTalk API (uniform client/server for HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, gRPC)
     |
     v
  Netty (event loop, NIO, connection management)
     |
     v
  OS sockets

HTTP server in ServiceTalk (sketch)

  HttpService service = (ctx, request) -> {
      ctx.sendOk().payload("hello");
  };

  HttpServers.forPort(8080)
      .listenAndAwait(service);

HTTP client in ServiceTalk

  StreamingHttpClient client = HttpClients.forSingleAddress("http://backend:8080")
                                          .buildStreaming();

  client.request(client.get("/health"))
        .toFuture()
        .thenAccept(response -> {
            if (response.status().code() == 200) { /* ok */ }
        });

Connection pooling (framework-managed)

  - HTTP/2: many streams per connection (multiplexing)
  - HTTP/1.1: pool + keep-alive
  - the application does not open connections directly

Load balancing (first-class)

  ServiceDiscovery disco = ...;
  HttpClients.forResolvedAddress(disco, "backend")
             .buildStreaming();
  // the client balances across discovered instances

Reactive body with backpressure

  Publisher<Buffer> body = request.payloadBody();
  // backpressure flows from consumer to producer automatically

Comparison to peers

  Spring MVC        blocking, HTTP/1.1-focused, big ecosystem
  Spring WebFlux    reactive, HTTP/1.1 + HTTP/2, Spring ecosystem
  gRPC-Java         gRPC-only (HTTP/2), protocol-locked
  ServiceTalk       HTTP/1.1 + HTTP/2 + gRPC unified, Netflix-scale
                    smaller ecosystem, lower-level, higher performance

