RPC shape

  // client looks local
  User u = userService.getUserById(42);

  // under the hood:
  // 1. stub serializes args -> bytes
  // 2. client sends request to server
  // 3. server deserializes, calls real method
  // 4. result serialized, sent back
  // 5. stub deserializes, returns to caller

  // failure modes are NOT like local calls:
  // - network timeout (unknown if server processed)
  // - partial failure (server crashed mid-operation)
  // - retry duplication (calling twice has side effects)
  // - version skew (client and server disagree on schema)

REST shape

  GET /users/42
  // HTTP semantics make failure explicit:
  // 200 OK, 404 Not Found, 503 Service Unavailable
  // idempotence and safety declared by method
  // cacheability declared by headers

Decision matrix

  Public API, browser client     -> REST
  Resource-oriented domain       -> REST
  Cacheable data                 -> REST
  Internal service-to-service    -> gRPC / Thrift
  Streaming (bidi, server push)  -> gRPC
  Polyglot clients               -> gRPC + Protobuf
  UI aggregation over many APIs  -> GraphQL

Fallacies of distributed computing (RPC invites all of these)

  1. The network is reliable
  2. Latency is zero
  3. Bandwidth is infinite
  4. The network is secure
  5. Topology does not change
  6. One administrator exists
  7. Transport cost is zero
  8. The network is homogeneous

